# CloudNativePG-compatible PostgreSQL operand image: PostGIS + GENUINE pgvector.
# Current build: PostgreSQL 18 (PostGIS 3.6, pgvector 0.8.7) for the pg-cluster-v18
# blue-green upgrade of 2026-10-15. The PG16 tag (16-pgvector0.8.0) stays in ghcr
# untouched as the rollback image of the old pg-cluster. Rebuilds publish a new
# dated tag (18-pgvector0.8.7-20261019 picked up the base's libexpat1 2.9.0 fix)
# and never overwrite one a cluster runs; see .github/workflows/pgvector-image.yml.
# Since 18-pgvector0.8.7-20261117, `vector` is a TRUSTED extension (see "WHY
# VECTOR IS TRUSTED" below); PostGIS stays superuser-only.
# =============================================================================
# WHY THIS IMAGE EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hybrid-recall dense leg stores embeddings in `memories.embedding halfvec(1024)`
# and queries them with `embedding <=> $1::halfvec` over an HNSW index built with
# `halfvec_cosine_ops` (see migrations/versions/005_add_embeddings_and_graph.py and
# src/claude_memory/api/recall.py). All of `halfvec`, the `<=>` cosine operator, the
# `hnsw` access method, and `halfvec_cosine_ops` are provided ONLY by pgvector.
#
# The shared CNPG cluster (dbaas/pg-cluster) currently runs the stock CNPG PostGIS
# operand image `ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/postgis:16`. That image bundles PostGIS but
# NOT pgvector — `pg_available_extensions` lists `postgis` and not `vector`, and
# `CREATE EXTENSION vector` fails with "extension \"vector\" is not available".
# Migration 005 is availability-gated, so on that image every vector step correctly
# NO-OPS and the dense leg can never function. This image is the operand-image swap
# that makes `vector` AVAILABLE so a re-run of migration 005 picks up the column +
# index. Promotion procedure: docs/runbooks/promote-pgvector-dense-recall.md.
#
# WHY VECTOR IS TRUSTED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloud (viktor/cloud) gives each Project a database on the shared cluster, owned
# by a p_* role that is NOT a superuser. PostgreSQL lets a non-superuser with
# CREATE on the database run `CREATE EXTENSION` only for extensions whose control
# file says `trusted = true`. Upstream pgvector does not set it, so without this a
# Project migration running `CREATE EXTENSION vector` fails with "permission
# denied". The build appends `trusted = true` to vector.control and fails if the
# line is missing afterwards. The pgvector 0.8.7 install script has no CREATE OR
# REPLACE and runs no statements at install time, only CREATE TYPE / FUNCTION /
# OPERATOR / CAST / AGGREGATE, and PostgreSQL 18 runs it with search_path set to
# the target schema, then pg_temp. pg_trgm and the other contrib extensions Cloud
# needs are already trusted in the base. PostGIS stays untrusted: the build fails
# if postgis.control ever says `trusted = true`. No SECURITY DEFINER wrapper is
# used instead, by decision for the shared cluster.
#
# WHY THIS BASE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# We extend the CNPG PostGIS operand image rather than a vanilla postgres image so:
#   * the CNPG instance manager + barman-cloud tooling the operator injects survive
#     (a vanilla `postgres:16` is NOT a valid CNPG operand image), and
#   * PostGIS is PRESERVED. The shared cluster is multi-tenant and at least one tenant
#     (dawarich) has `postgis` installed; dropping PostGIS would break it. This image
#     is strictly additive: PostGIS stays, pgvector is added.
#
# THE TRAP WE EXPLICITLY AVOID  (read before "improving" this file)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pgvecto.rs / VectorChord is a DIFFERENT vector engine. It ships a `vectors.so`
# library loaded via `shared_preload_libraries=vectors.so` and exposes the `vectors`
# / `vchord` extensions — NOT `vector`. It does NOT provide `CREATE EXTENSION vector`,
# the `halfvec` type, `halfvec_cosine_ops`, or the `hnsw` access method this codebase
# needs. An image built on pgvecto.rs would leave migration 005 silently in its no-op
# (lexical-only) state with NO error. So this Dockerfile:
#   * installs ONLY the genuine pgvector Debian package `postgresql-16-pgvector`,
#   * does NOT add the pgvecto.rs binary, and
#   * does NOT set `shared_preload_libraries=vectors.so` (pgvector needs no preload;
#     `CREATE EXTENSION vector` alone is sufficient). We do NOT override the base
#     image's ENTRYPOINT/CMD — CNPG drives the operand via its own instance manager,
#     and `postgresql.parameters` in the Cluster spec is the only place server params
#     belong.
#
# BUILD / PUBLISH
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per infra ADR-0002, first-party images build on GitHub Actions and publish to ghcr.
# Build for linux/amd64 (cluster arch), then set the `pg_cluster_image` Terraform
# variable to the pushed tag per the runbook. Example (CI does this; shown for clarity):
#   docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 \
#     -f deploy/infra/Dockerfile.pgvector \
#     -t ghcr.io/viktorbarzin/cnpg-postgis-pgvector:18-pgvector0.8.7 --push deploy/infra
# (Pin the pgvector version via the build-arg below so the tag is reproducible.)

# Base: CNPG's PostGIS operand image for PG 18.6 / PostGIS 3.6.4 on Debian trixie, "standard" flavour
# (the "system" flavour that bundled barman-cloud is deprecated; this cluster has no
# object-store backup configured, so nothing here needs barman-cloud).
ARG PG_MAJOR=18
FROM ghcr.io/cloudnative-pg/postgis:18.6-3.6.4-standard-trixie
ARG PG_MAJOR

# Pin the pgvector version so rebuilds are reproducible. 0.8.x ships the halfvec type,
# the hnsw access method, and halfvec_cosine_ops — the exact surface migration 005
# and recall.py depend on.
ARG PGVECTOR_VERSION=0.8.7

# Install the GENUINE pgvector extension from the PostgreSQL Debian repository the
# PostGIS operand image already trusts. `postgresql-<major>-pgvector` is the Debian
# package name for pgvector (trixie-pgdg). The version gate below fails the build if
# the repository has moved past PGVECTOR_VERSION; bump the ARG and the tag together. NOTHING about pgvecto.rs/VectorChord is added.
# The base already ships postgresql-18-pgvector 0.8.7 (pgdg13+1, seen 2026-11-17).
# --no-upgrade keeps that package as it is: the newer pgdg13+2 build depends on
# postgresql-18 (>> 18.7), so a plain install would also move the server from
# 18.6 to 18.7, and a server minor change is its own decision, not a side effect.
# Switch to root for apt, install, then drop back to the postgres uid the base runs as.
USER root
RUN set -eux; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends --no-upgrade "postgresql-${PG_MAJOR}-pgvector"; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    # Sanity-gate the build: the vector and postgis control files MUST be present,
    # and the server major must match, otherwise we have silently produced an image
    # that cannot CREATE EXTENSION vector / postgis.
    ext=/usr/share/postgresql/${PG_MAJOR}/extension; \
    test -f "$ext/vector.control"; \
    test -f "$ext/postgis.control"; \
    grep -q "default_version = '${PGVECTOR_VERSION}'" "$ext/vector.control"; \
    /usr/lib/postgresql/${PG_MAJOR}/bin/postgres --version | grep -q "(PostgreSQL) ${PG_MAJOR}\."; \
    # Mark vector trusted (replace any existing trusted line, then append) so a
    # non-superuser database owner can CREATE EXTENSION vector. Gate both ways:
    # vector must say trusted = true exactly once, postgis must not be trusted.
    sed -i '/^[[:space:]]*trusted[[:space:]]*=/d' "$ext/vector.control"; \
    echo "trusted = true" >> "$ext/vector.control"; \
    test "$(grep -cx 'trusted = true' "$ext/vector.control")" = 1; \
    if grep -Eq '^[[:space:]]*trusted[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*true' "$ext/postgis.control"; then \
      echo "postgis.control is trusted; PostGIS must stay superuser-only" >&2; exit 1; \
    fi

# 26 == the postgres user in the CNPG operand images; matches the base image default.
USER 26
